 {:db/id "b7510f5f-cbaf-5458-b2ee-010c1ddc98af"
  :question/id #uuid "b7510f5f-cbaf-5458-b2ee-010c1ddc98af"
  :question/problem "**(b)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle y,x\\rangle$, find a potential function $\\phi$ and a stream function $\\psi$. Draw a couple of equipotential curves and streamlines.\n\n![Source vector-field diagram](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Ffield-1b.png)"
  :question/answer-fields ["458f209f-05f5-5241-98e9-84a208df632c"]
  :question/worked-solution "Check $\\nabla\\phi=(y,x)$, $\\psi_y=y$, and $-\\psi_x=x$. The roles of the two hyperbola families are exchanged relative to part (a), with the indicated sign change in the stream function."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "e8acfb86-65b6-5e64-a923-778070420c77"
  :answer/id #uuid "e8acfb86-65b6-5e64-a923-778070420c77"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "On the punctured plane, $\\phi=\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2)=\\ln r$ is a global potential. A stream function is locally $\\psi=\\theta=\\arg(x+iy)$ after choosing an angle branch. No single-valued global stream function exists on the full punctured plane.\n\nEquipotentials are circles $r=e^c$; streamlines are outward radial rays $\\theta=k$. Draw a couple of circles and a couple of rays, excluding the origin.\n\n**Authored reference sketch:**\n\n![Equipotentials and streamlines for part c](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-5%2Fauthored-curves-1c.png)"
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
