 {:db/id "80878db9-2bc3-5918-ad59-784bc38c3c1e"
  :question/id #uuid "80878db9-2bc3-5918-ad59-784bc38c3c1e"
  :question/problem "**(d)** Show that on the left edge of $R$, $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=-f(a)$."
  :question/answer-fields ["99d19a61-6d40-56fa-a974-3fee4ccc5bf6"]
  :question/worked-solution "Counterclockwise traversal keeps the rectangle on the left. On the left edge this requires a downward tangent, accounting for the negative sign."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "7f5f4c85-8d0a-5cbf-ad66-eb65561b455e"
  :answer/id #uuid "7f5f4c85-8d0a-5cbf-ad66-eb65561b455e"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "Under Green's usual regularity hypotheses, for example if $f\\in C^1([a,b])$, the boundary calculation from (b–d) gives\n\n$$f(b)-f(a)=\\oint_{\\partial R}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\iint_R f'(x)\\,dA=\\int_a^b\\int_0^1f'(x)\\,dy\\,dx=\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,dx.$$\n\nThis is the **Fundamental Theorem of Calculus**, in its Newton–Leibniz form.\n\nThe source assumes only that $f$ is differentiable. That alone does not ensure continuous first partial derivatives, as required by the standard version of Green's theorem used here; the formal derivation needs the additional regularity condition above (or an appropriate generalized theorem)."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
