 {:db/id "cbd43edd-2d6a-5c69-a008-f7a6c7c2cfb5"
  :question/id #uuid "cbd43edd-2d6a-5c69-a008-f7a6c7c2cfb5"
  :question/problem "**(a)** Find the area enclosed by the cardioid. **Source hint:** Use Green's theorem with $\\mathbf F=\\langle0,x\\rangle$.\n\nArea: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["f5051d80-9e5f-57a7-93fe-c6bd449a469f"]
  :question/worked-solution "For $\\mathbf F=(0,x)$, the scalar curl is $1$, so Green's theorem gives $A=\\oint_C x\\,dy$. Write $\\rho(t)=1-\\sin t$, $x=\\rho\\cos t$, and $y=\\rho\\sin t$. The traversal is counterclockwise. Since $\\oint_Cd(xy)=0$,\n\n$$A=\\frac12\\oint_C(x\\,dy-y\\,dx)=\\frac12\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\rho(t)^2\\,dt.$$\n\nHere $xy'-yx'=\\rho^2$, so\n\n$$A=\\frac12\\int_0^{2\\pi}(1-2\\sin t+\\sin^2t)\\,dt=\\frac12(2\\pi+\\pi)=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.$$\n\nThis derives the polar-area expression from the requested Green's-theorem field rather than substituting a different required method."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
