 {:db/id "d804dc20-c6fb-53e1-8c66-8b204ed8bbc7"
  :question/id #uuid "d804dc20-c6fb-53e1-8c66-8b204ed8bbc7"
  :question/problem "**(b)** Find the arc length of the cardioid.\n\n**Source hint:**\n\n$$\\sqrt{1-\\sin t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1-\\sin t}\\sqrt{1+\\sin t}}{\\sqrt{1+\\sin t}}=\\frac{|\\cos t|}{\\sqrt{1+\\sin t}}.$$\n\nArc length: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["4e09c81b-8f8b-51d0-9506-bfe9be9debce"]
  :question/worked-solution "With $\\rho=1-\\sin t$ and $\\rho'=-\\cos t$, the speed satisfies\n\n$$\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{(\\rho')^2+\\rho^2}=\\sqrt{\\cos^2t+(1-\\sin t)^2}=\\sqrt{2(1-\\sin t)}.$$\n\nUsing the supplied hint, split where $\\cos t$ changes sign: $t=\\pi/2$ and $3\\pi/2$. On the three resulting intervals, integrate $\\sqrt2|\\cos t|/\\sqrt{1+\\sin t}$ to obtain respectively $4-2\\sqrt2$, $4$, and $2\\sqrt2$. Their sum is $8$.\n\nThe quotient in the hint is undefined at $t=3\\pi/2$, even though the original speed is defined there; use the continuous limiting value in this intermediate expression. Alternatively, the identity $\\sqrt{2(1-\\sin t)}=2|\\sin(t/2-\\pi/4)|$ yields the same total length."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
